          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have coronary artery disease.",
          "answer": "If you have new, worsening, or rest-onset chest pain or pressure, call emergency services now. Do not wait to see if it passes. The red-flag guidance (Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack) says chest discomfort in someone with known coronary artery disease is treated as a possible heart attack until proven otherwise. Pressure or tightness lasting more than a few minutes, pain spreading to the arms, jaw or back, or chest discomfort with breathlessness, sweating, nausea or light-headedness all need emergency care.\n\nCoronary artery disease is a build-up of plaque in the arteries that supply the heart muscle (Coronary artery disease, general clinical reference). It can cause predictable chest discomfort on exertion, and it can also cause sudden clots when plaque ruptures. Because of the second mechanism, a past normal stress test does not rule out a heart attack now.\n\nThe secondary prevention guidance (Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis) says that a pattern of angina that is becoming more frequent, easier to provoke, or longer-lasting is a change that needs urgent assessment. Tell your clinician promptly if this happens.\n\nThe search results did not cover treatment options, medicines, or what your own care plan should look like. Please discuss those with your clinician. If you tell me what you would like to understand better, I can search the guidance again for that topic.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
