          "trace_id": "26d11581f23f4baeb0fa429db8e6310b",
          "session_id": "full-mt-m-015",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is meant by secondary prevention?",
          "answer": "Secondary prevention means the steps taken by someone who already has established coronary artery disease, such as after a heart attack, a procedure to open the arteries, or a diagnosis on imaging. The aim is to lower the chance of another event. (Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis)\n\nIt is a package of measures, not one treatment. The guidance says the benefit comes from using them together, and that the common failure is dropping parts of the package, often the non-drug parts. The guidance names antiplatelet therapy and high-intensity statin therapy as components. (Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis)\n\nThe general reference lists risk factors that can be changed, including smoking, high blood pressure, raised LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, and diet. Age, sex, and family history cannot be changed. (Coronary artery disease, general clinical reference)\n\nThe passages I found did not describe the full list of components, the doses, or which medicines suit you. Please ask your clinician to go through the complete plan with you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
